0. Simple web stack - www.foobar.com

Flow: a user types www.foobar.com in the browser. DNS resolves the name to 8.8.8.8,
the browser opens a TCP/IP connection to that server and sends an HTTP request.
Nginx receives it, serves static files directly and forwards dynamic requests to the
application server, which runs the code base and queries MySQL. The response goes
back the same way to the user.

Specifics:
- Server: a physical or virtual machine that provides services (here: web, app and
  database) to clients over the network.
- Domain name: a human-readable alias (foobar.com) for the server's IP address, so
  users do not need to remember 8.8.8.8.
- DNS record for www in www.foobar.com: an A record, mapping www.foobar.com -> 8.8.8.8.
- Web server (Nginx): handles HTTP requests, serves static content (HTML, CSS,
  images) and passes dynamic requests to the application server.
- Application server: runs the application code (business logic) and builds dynamic
  responses.
- Application files: the code base executed by the application server.
- Database (MySQL): stores and retrieves the application's data.
- Communication with the user's computer: TCP/IP, using HTTP (port 80).

Issues:
- SPOF: there is only one server. If it goes down, the whole site is down.
- Downtime during maintenance: deploying new code or restarting the web server
  makes the site unavailable for that time.
- Cannot scale: one server has limited CPU, RAM and bandwidth. With too much
  incoming traffic it slows down or crashes, and there is nowhere to send the
  extra load.
